17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416060 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4c18e7a-0cb9-5985-b003-006e3604014c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416061 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416061 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416061 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416061 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416061 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ed66a49d-bd94-53a1-92eb-bd214fe6b1b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={1,2,3}.$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18473aee-7220-58ca-a3d1-b01636850f3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416062 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $x+y=4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,3),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,1),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={1,2,4}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416063 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe1e7ad055ab47eca3234dfc3797a7316c554868a62cb4e46a303d369baeeaa0.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $y\\le {x}^{2}.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1),$ and we should add the following arrow to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n1\\to 1\n$$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1),\\,(2,2),\\,(2,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n2\\to 1,\\,2\\to 2,\\,2\\to 4\n$$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,1),$ $(4,2),$ $(4,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n4\\to 1,\\,4\\to 2,\\,4\\to 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${2,5,8}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(2,8)$\n- $(8,5)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416064 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/38df1bcf68146317ae6450e495658203a6427551d0510bd232c8cbfe5b82d386.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(w,w),(w,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,x),(z,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57a0e862-6493-53ed-a45b-3b4d586c00e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416065 #edb/ref 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $w:$\n\n- $w\\to w$ and $w\\to z,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(w,w)$ and $(w,z).$\n\n- No arrows start from $x.$\n\n- Arrows starting from $y:$\n\n- $y\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(y,x).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $z:$\n\n- $z\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(z,x).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(w,w),(w,z),(y,x),(z,x)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416066 #edb/ref 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n- $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n- $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416067 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\n