x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416069 #edb/ref 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416070 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set goes along the horizontal line,\n\n- the second set goes along the vertical line,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nIn our case, the plot contains all the points in ${R}^{2}$ for which \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81.\n$$\n\nThese are the points that lie *inside* the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin.\n\nFinally, since we have a strict inequality, the points on the circle must be excluded from the plot, which we depict by using a *dashed* curve:\n\n![](images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A={Z,Q,R}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416071 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/98/983be4b904bbbf72f896c2e96d3691c74179c9e078494d581fb8c9b2c0c64b7f.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $Y⊂X.$\n\n- If $X=Z,$ then the value $Y=Z$ satisfies $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(Z,Z),$ and we should add the following arrow  to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nZ\\to Z\n$$\n\n- If $X=Q,$ then the values $Y=Z,Q$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(Q,Z),(Q,Q),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nQ\\to Z,\\,Q\\to Q\n$$\n\n- If $X=R,$ then the values $y=Z,Q,R$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(R,Z),$ $(R,Q),$ $(R,R),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nR\\to Z,\\,R\\to Q,\\,R\\to R\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X={2,4,6}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(4,4)$\n- $(6,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416072 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419018 #edb/ref 1080 "images/90/90f18f9bc042dda952e423a56268bd6b917db2f94f9a7b42ee8087290b169fa4.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419018 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419018 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45d65c90-dfbc-57d3-96ca-a717e1f395a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419019 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b79f667-cd57-53d0-854e-fda5aee2821d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419020 #edb/ref 1080 "images/7b/7bac8457b66917fc1aa9866c04fddef3685196f48d68cd9ce40642b144cb41aa.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d174cbc8-3270-5fcf-b235-c7e8a0ccc0cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419021 #edb/ref 1080 "images/3a/3a95797dc283876f7b5c4d71c1f954481cb5d21caa7a1c5acbdaec952991c84f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f24e9d57-53f5-569d-babd-8bd097337ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419022 #edb/re